# November 24, 2026: northern Lewis County and the Tug Hill edge, region nny (North Country & St. Lawrence). Asked for the
# most obscure ground in New York, the session counted the Atlas's places by half-degree square and took the emptiest one
# with old buildings in it: the Black River valley round Lowville and Croghan and the Tug Hill towns west of it, where the
# Atlas had four places within 25 km of Montague and none in Lowville, Croghan, Beaver Falls, Pinckney or Osceola.
# Southern Lewis County was done in batch mohw_iugo (Constableville, Lyons Falls, Port Leyden, Martinsburg).
# Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia as raw text, the 2024 National Register nomination of the Lowville & Beaver
# River Railroad Historic District (parks.ny.gov; its first half read word for word, including the resource-by-resource
# inventory with a coordinate for each), the Pinckney Corners Cemetery nomination (NPGallery), NPGallery's record pages,
# historypointer.com (Register addresses), the Pomeroy Foundation's marker pages, HMdb, the Lewis County Historical
# Society (historicallylewis.org), the Adirondack Mennonite Heritage Farm's own pages, NYGenWeb's Lewis County pages,
# North Country Heritage (TAUNY), alpsroads.net, Adirondack Explorer, waymarking.com, the Town of Osceola and Tug Hill
# Council pages, Lewis County's rail-trail pages and the ArcGIS geocoder.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'nny_tughill_bmwh','label':'Northern Lewis County & Tug Hill: Lowville, Croghan, Beaver Falls, Pinckney','added':'2026-11-24 02:17'}
LBR=L('National Register nomination: Lowville & Beaver River Railroad Historic District (2024, PDF)','https://parks.ny.gov/documents/shpo/national-register/LowvilleandBeaverRiverRailroadLowvilletoCroghanLewisCounty.pdf')
LBRM=L('Pomeroy Foundation: Lowville & Beaver River Railroad marker','https://www.wgpfoundation.org/historic-markers/lowville-beaver-river-railroad-historic-district/')
TRAIL=L('Lewis County: rail trail initiative','https://lewiscountyny.gov/railtrailinitiative/')
LCHS=L('Lewis County Historical Society: museums and societies','https://historicallylewis.org/heritage-tourism/local-museums-libraries-and-historical-societies')
HP=lambda n,r:L('historypointer: '+n,'https://historypointer.com/nris/resource/%s'%r)
PLACES=[
N('croghan-depot-lbr','Croghan Depot and Turntable, Lowville & Beaver River Railroad','Croghan','nny','rail','standing',43.89387,-75.39311,'exact','open','drive','stations','1904–2007',
  'The far end of the Lowville & Beaver River Railroad, a 10.6-mile short line built between 1903 and 1906 from Lowville through New Bremen and Beaver Falls to Croghan, chiefly to serve the Lewis family\'s paper mills at Beaver Falls. It opened in January 1906, ran steam until 1957 and freight until the Beaver Falls mill closed in January 2007, and was placed on the National Register as a historic district in 2024. The 1904 depot is the home of the Railway Historical Society of Northern New York\'s museum.',
  'A long wood-frame depot (22 by 100 feet) on a river-rock foundation, with horizontal wood siding and a side-gable roof; the 2024 nomination calls its integrity excellent, and a writer for Adirondack Explorer found the lobby much as it was when built. Just east are the line\'s 1907 concrete turntable pit (51 feet, "1907" cast in the concrete) and the concrete base of the water tower; a 1990s steel engine house stands on the footprint of the old wooden one. Outside the depot stand a 1918 Lima Shay geared locomotive, a 1909 wooden snowplow and a motor car the society uses for occasional speeder rides. Interior not checked.',
  'The end of the line, with its turntable still in the ground.',
  'Railway Historical Society of Northern New York, 9781 NY 812 as you enter the village. An undated NYGenWeb page for the society gives Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. and Sunday 1 to 4 p.m., a $2 donation ($1 children and seniors), bus tours by appointment and no trains running; Wikipedia says to contact the staff before you come. Those hours were not confirmed for this year. The track beyond the yard is not a trail: Lewis County bought the line in November 2025 to make one, and its page said no construction had begun. Village lot on Bank Street, 60 m away.',
  [W('Lowville and Beaver River Railroad'),LBR,LBRM,L('NYGenWeb: the Railway Historical Society of Northern New York','https://www.nygenweb.net/lewis/Organisations/RHSNNY/rhsnny.html'),L('Adirondack Explorer: speeder rides at Croghan','https://adirondackexplorer.org/?p=286782'),TRAIL],
  th=[43.89363,-75.39272],thn='Bank Street lot, Croghan',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('lowville-lbr-yard','Lowville & Beaver River Railroad yard: dining house, engine houses and turntable','Lowville','nny','rail','standing',43.78774,-75.48695,'site','view','drive','stations','about 1880–2007',
  'The Lowville end of the 1906 short line to Croghan, where it met the New York Central (later the Mohawk, Adirondack & Northern). The 2024 Register nomination counts here a brick building of about 1880 with a T-shaped plan that served as the line\'s depot and dining house, the New York Central\'s own station of about 1912–1920, a wooden engine house of about 1938 (its 1912 predecessor burned that year), a concrete-block engine house of about 1950 that still shelters the line\'s 1947 General Electric 44-ton diesel, a 55-foot Armstrong turntable of 1905, a water-tower base, an oil and sand shed and the spur into the shop yard.',
  'The brick dining house with its segmental arches and stone lintels, the stuccoed Central station (owned by a building-supply cooperative, some windows filled in), the two engine houses and the turntable, all within about 200 m. The nomination found them in good order in 2024. Interiors not checked.',
  'A small railroad\'s whole yard, engine and turntable included, left as it was.',
  'Railroad and private business property: look from the public streets. The right-of-way belonged to the Genesee Valley Transportation Company and Lewis County when the nomination was written; the county bought the line in November 2025 for a future trail, with no construction begun. Do not walk the tracks or enter the yard. The street to stand on was not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Lowville and Beaver River Railroad'),LBR,TRAIL],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('black-river-trestle-lbr','Lowville & Beaver River Railroad bridges over the Black River','New Bremen','nny','rail','standing',43.82196,-75.46468,'site','view','drive','','1905–1910',
  'Where the short line from Lowville to Croghan crossed the Black River and its flood flats: a steel truss bridge of 1905, 286 feet long, over the river itself, and a steel trestle of 1910, about 1,000 feet long on fourteen concrete piers, built by the American Bridge Company across the flats. Wikipedia describes the river crossing differently, as a 1,100-foot Warren swing through truss; the Register nomination\'s figures are given here. No trains have crossed since the Beaver Falls mill closed in 2007.',
  'The steel truss and the long trestle run beside the NY 812 bridges over the Black River, so both can be seen from the highway. The nomination of 2024 lists both as contributing and intact.',
  'A thousand feet of trestle over the Black River flats, beside a state highway.',
  'Railroad property, owned by Lewis County since its purchase of November 2025 for a future rail trail (no construction yet). Look from the NY 812 bridges, which are open to walkers and cyclists in OpenStreetMap; NY 812 is a fast road, so stop only where it is safe. Do not walk onto the trestle.',
  [W('Lowville and Beaver River Railroad'),LBR,TRAIL],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('beaver-falls-trestle-depot-lbr','Beaver Falls trestle and depot, Lowville & Beaver River Railroad','Beaver Falls','nny','rail','standing',43.88434,-75.43191,'site','view','drive','stations','1905–1912',
  'At Beaver Falls the short line crossed the falls of the Beaver River on a steel and wood trestle of 1912, about 1,100 feet long on nine concrete piers, and served the paper mills the railroad was built for. Its 1905 depot, a wooden building 22 by 70 feet with a gable roof, stands about 500 m southeast and was in 2024 the office of a private water-treatment chemical maker. The mill closed in January 2007 and the trains stopped.',
  'The long trestle over the river and falls, and the plain wooden depot, which the 2024 Register nomination found to keep its outside largely unchanged. The nomination also notes washouts on the line near Beaver Falls. Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'Railroad property (Lewis County bought the line in November 2025 for a future trail) and a private business in the depot: look from Main Street (County Route 8) and the Cutoff Road bridge. Do not walk the trestle or the tracks. Where best to see the trestle from a public road was not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Lowville and Beaver River Railroad'),LBR,TRAIL],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('croghan-island-mill','Croghan Island Mill','Croghan','nny','industry','standing',43.89837,-75.39261,'exact','view','drive','','before 1840',
  'A water-powered mill on an island in the Beaver River at Croghan. North Country Heritage (TAUNY) says it was built some time before 1840, that a log crib dam of 1848 was replaced by the present double dam about 1906, and that it made window sashes, blinds and other millwork; it calls it the oldest water-powered lumber mill in the state still working, run by the Martin family. The Register listing of 2010 gives 1875 and 1906 as its key years. The same page says the state took stop logs out of the dam and that if the dam goes, the mill will likely no longer run on water power.',
  'A clapboarded gable-roofed mill of three blocks (main mill, cold storage and office) with its wheel house and flume box, and the 1906 concrete dam with a drop of about two stories (Wikipedia). TAUNY says much of the machinery is a century old and works. alpsroads.net describes the old 1875 bridge to the island, rebuilt in 1952 and now bypassed, and the dam\'s ruins seen from South Bridge Street. Whether the mill still runs, and whether it ever shows visitors round, was not found. Interior not checked.',
  'A working water-powered mill on an island, with its dam.',
  'Private, a working business at 9897 South Bridge Street (Register address): look from South Bridge Street and the village streets. Mapped lots on Bridge Street and Main Street, both about 130 m away; who owns those lots was not checked.',
  [W('Croghan Island Mill'),L('North Country Heritage (TAUNY): Croghan Island Mill','https://ncha.tauny.org/?p=1520'),L('alpsroads.net: Croghan Island Mill Bridge','https://www.alpsroads.net/roads/ny/croghan/'),HP('Croghan Island Mill','10000515')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('mennonite-heritage-farm-croghan','Adirondack Mennonite Heritage Farm (Moser Farm)','Croghan','nny','landmark','standing',43.89224,-75.33418,'exact','open','drive','','1845–',
  'The farm of the Moser family, who came from Alsace-Lorraine in the 1830s and kept it until 1982. Its 1845 farmhouse served for about three generations as one of the homes where the Amish Mennonite congregation took turns to worship, before the Croghan Mennonite church was built in 1912. Bought by a heritage group in 1990 as an open-air museum, it was listed on the National Register in 2010 as the Moser Farm.',
  'The 1845 frame farmhouse (two-story main block and a long ell), a granary of the mid to late 1800s and two English barns of 1874, all close together. Inside the house are displays of the community\'s worship before 1912 and an archive; the granary holds a buggy, wagons and maple-sugaring tools, and visitors may walk through the 1874 barn. There are graves of the first settlers on the farm and a nature trail of about three miles (dedicated 2022; maps in the gift shop). A Pomeroy roadside marker went up in 2016.',
  'The farmhouse that was a church for three generations.',
  'Run by the Adirondack Mennonite Heritage Association, 8778 Erie Canal Road near Kirschnerville. Admission by donation; group tours with a week\'s notice, and visits on other days by appointment (the farm\'s Visit page, read November 24, 2026). Telephone 315-346-1122. No mapped lot; parking at the farm was not checked.',
  [W('Moser Farm'),L('Adirondack Mennonite Heritage Farm','https://mennoniteheritagefarm.com'),L('Adirondack Mennonite Heritage Farm: Visit','https://mennoniteheritagefarm.com/post.php?pid=3'),L('NYGenWeb: the Mennonite Heritage Farm','https://www.nygenweb.net/lewis/Museums/MennoniteHeritageFarm/m1ennonite.html'),LCHS],
  brick='no',hours='July to October: Monday and Wednesday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; July and August also Saturday 10 a.m. to 3 p.m.; other days by appointment'),
N('beaver-falls-grange-hall-554','Beaver Falls Grange Hall No. 554','Beaver Falls','nny','civic','standing',43.88577,-75.43089,'exact','view','drive','','1892',
  'A Grange hall built in 1892 as the meeting place and entertainment hall of a small farming community. Its first floor became a store in 1915 and a meat market was added in the basement in 1923; the last shop left in the mid-1980s. The 2015 Register nomination found it empty: the Grange was still active but small and no longer met in its hall. Listed on the National Register in 2015; a Pomeroy marker followed in 2016.',
  'A two-story wood-frame hall 30 by 60 feet in narrow clapboards on an irregular fieldstone foundation, gable end to the street, with a one-story hipped porch across the front. Upstairs, the nomination describes the Grange Room with its stage and a painted 1920s advertising curtain, an anteroom with a peephole door, a kitchen and a gallery above it for the Juvenile Grange; the first floor is one open room with its store fixtures gone. Whether any of that can be seen now was not checked.',
  'A Grange hall with its painted stage curtain still upstairs.',
  'Owned by Beaver Falls Grange No. 554 when the 2015 nomination was written, and empty then; look from Main Street (9577 Main Street). Not open to visitors so far as any source says.',
  [W('Beaver Falls Grange Hall No. 554'),L('National Register nomination: Beaver Falls Grange Hall (NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/8df0d7a0-3121-4c69-8778-75c604ff1cb2'),L('Pomeroy Foundation: Beaver Falls Grange Hall marker','https://www.wgpfoundation.org/historic-markers/beaver-falls-grange-hall'),HP('Beaver Falls Grange Hall No. 554','15000852')],
  brick='no'),
N('lewis-house-beaver-falls','Harry and Molly Lewis House (Fiber Products Research Center)','Beaver Falls','nny','landmark','standing',43.88453,-75.42643,'site','view','drive','','1909–1910',
  'The house of Harry and Molly Lewis of the paper-making family whose mills made Beaver Falls, built in 1909–10 in the Colonial Revival style. In 1957 it became the Fiber Products Research Center of the J.P. Lewis paper company. Listed on the National Register in 2011.',
  'A two-and-a-half-story, five-bay masonry house with intersecting hipped roofs and a two-story portico, designed by Charles Wisner & Sons (NPGallery), with its garage, workshop and water system of the same years (Wikipedia). Use today and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private, at 9520 East Main Street (Register address); look from the road. Owner and use today not found.',
  [W('Harry and Molly Lewis House'),HP('Harry and Molly Lewis House','12000956')],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('pinckney-corners-cemetery','Pinckney Corners Cemetery','Pinckney','nny','burial','traces',43.86755,-75.72477,'site','public','drive','cemeteries','1810–1901',
  'The oldest burying ground of the Tug Hill town of Pinckney, begun in 1810, with about 240 marked graves from 1810 to 1901, among them two veterans of the Revolution and twelve of the War of 1812 (Register nomination). It is nearly all that is left of Pinckney Corners: an 1873 gazetteer gave the place a Methodist Episcopal church, a blacksmith and wagon shop and about eight houses, and the church across the road is gone. Listed on the National Register in 2014.',
  'About half an acre of early stones in rows, with no fence and no paths. The 2013 survey for the nomination found it overgrown and many stones toppled, and the town had begun repairs with a contractor; how it looks now was not checked.',
  'A Tug Hill crossroads that left only its graves.',
  'Owned by the Town of Pinckney (Register nomination), on the north side of Pinckney Road just west of County Route 194. No parking area; pull well off the road. Walk carefully among fallen stones and do not move them.',
  [W('Pinckney Corners Cemetery'),L('National Register nomination: Pinckney Corners Cemetery (NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/daa49fd1-bbb8-4b06-ad46-5dceee56c696'),HP('Pinckney Corners Cemetery','14000578')],
  brick='no'),
N('lowville-stone-church','Lowville Presbyterian Church (the Stone Church)','Lowville','nny','worship','standing',43.79138,-75.49590,'site','view','drive','','1831',
  'The First Presbyterian Church of Lowville, built of stone in 1831 after two earlier churches burned within a short time (the Pomeroy marker says two years, HMdb\'s copy says three). Ezra Brainard was the builder. A bell came in 1860, a pipe organ some twenty years later, stained glass, a choir loft and a 1906 wing early in the 1900s, and the three-faced town clock in its wooden tower in 1911. Listed on the National Register in 2007.',
  'A rectangular stone church with a pedimented front pavilion and a staged wooden bell tower carrying the town clock, and the 1906 wing. A Pomeroy Foundation marker of 2013 stands at North State Street and Bostwick Street. What original woodwork survives inside, and the service times, were not checked.',
  '',
  'An active church at 7707 North State Street (the congregation\'s own page was not read); look from the street.',
  [W('Lowville Presbyterian Church'),L('Pomeroy Foundation: Stone Church marker','https://wgpfoundation.org/historic-markers/stone-church'),L('HMdb: Stone Church','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=80308'),L('NPGallery record','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/07000623')],
  brick='no'),
N('franklin-hough-house-lowville','Franklin B. Hough House','Lowville','nny','landmark','standing',43.78843,-75.50000,'exact','view','drive','','1860–1861',
  'The home of Franklin B. Hough, doctor, historian and the first federal official put in charge of the nation\'s forests (1876), whose office grew into the U.S. Forest Service; he is called the father of American forestry. The house was finished in 1861; Wikipedia says he lived here from 1863 to 1865 and that it stayed in the family until 1967. A National Historic Landmark since 1963.',
  'A two-and-a-half-story brick house set well back from Collins Street up a half-circle drive, with a truncated hip roof crowned by a rectangular belvedere, a gabled centre bay and heavy segmental hoods over the windows. A plaque marks the lot. Interior not checked.',
  'Where the U.S. Forest Service began, in a quiet Lowville street.',
  'Private house at 7629 Collins Street; look from the street. Present owner not found.',
  [W('Franklin B. Hough House'),HP('Franklin B. Hough House','66000526')],
  brick='?'),
N('lowville-masonic-temple-lchs','Lowville Masonic Temple (Lewis County Historical Society)','Lowville','nny','civic','standing',43.78597,-75.49141,'exact','open','drive','','1928',
  'A Colonial Revival brick lodge built in 1928 for Lowville\'s Masons. The Lewis County Historical Society bought it in 2002 and keeps its museum, research room and offices there. Listed on the National Register in 2012.',
  'The 1928 lodge outside and in, with the county\'s history collections. Interior details not checked.',
  '',
  'Lewis County Historical Society, 7552 South State Street. Admission free; free parking (the society\'s Location page). Groups by arrangement, telephone 315-376-8957 (the society\'s Hours & Admission page, read November 24, 2026).',
  [W('Lowville Masonic Temple'),L('Lewis County Historical Society: Hours & Admission','https://historicallylewis.org/?p=861'),L('Lewis County Historical Society: Location','https://historicallylewis.org/?p=859'),L('Lewis County Historical Society: Our Facilities','https://historicallylewis.org/?p=863')],
  brick='yes',hours='Tuesday to Friday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; often Mondays too in summer'),
N('bateman-hotel-lowville','Bateman Hotel','Lowville','nny','hotel','standing',43.78630,-75.49160,'site','view','drive','','1869',
  'A four-story Italianate and Second Empire hotel built in 1869, after a fire in January of that year burned downtown Lowville; it was also the Howell and the Kellogg. It is the centre of a unified brick commercial block that dominates State Street. Run by Harold and Marion Mahar from the 1950s, it closed as a hotel when "Mother Mahar" retired in the early 1980s, and is now condominiums. Listed on the National Register in 1994.',
  'Round-arched upper windows with yellow brick coursing over their heads, a bracketed cornice, a Colonial Revival porte-cochère of about 1909, and painted signs on the brick: "The Bateman" on the front between the second and third floors, with the older "Kellogg House" showing under it, and the same pair on the rear wing. The domed corner cupolas came off about 1960. Inside, the nomination describes a four-story spiral stair and an 1876 checkered marble floor; whether any of it is open to the public was not checked. A livery barn of about 1874 stands behind.',
  'Two hotel names painted one over the other.',
  'Private condominiums with businesses on the ground floor, 7574 South State Street; look from the street.',
  [W('Bateman Hotel (Lowville, New York)')],
  brick='yes'),
N('lewis-county-soldiers-monument','Lewis County Soldiers\' and Sailors\' Monument','Lowville','nny','curio','standing',43.79093,-75.49559,'exact','public','drive','','1883',
  'The county\'s Civil War monument, made in 1883 by the Monumental Bronze Company of Bridgeport, Connecticut: a 20-foot column of sand-cast zinc ("white bronze") on a three-stepped base, with a soldier on top. Listed on the National Register in 2009.',
  'The zinc soldier on his column, about 20 feet tall. Zinc monuments of this kind ring hollow when tapped; do not climb it.',
  '',
  'Stands in public view in the centre of Lowville, about 50 m from the Stone Church; who owns the ground was not checked.',
  [W("Lewis County Soldiers' and Sailors' Monument")],
  brick='no'),
]
